// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row unsized_dyn_local_binds_deref_admits (tier 2). AN ILLEGAL PROGRAM IS ADMITTED AND RUNS.
// A LOCAL WHOSE ANNOTATED TYPE IS A BARE TRAIT OBJECT — `let y: dyn Tr = *x;` where `x: &dyn Tr` — is accepted
// with no diagnostic, links, and RUNS exit 0. rustc REFUSES it: E0277, the size for values of type `dyn Tr`
// cannot be known at compilation time; a local must have a statically known size.
//
// MEASURED 2026-09-16 (round 2026-09-16l-refptee) on base 53884ccdf5a135ca 43, the UNARMED binary, through
// tests/logos/run_test.sh pass mode: logosc rc 0, binary exit 0.
//
// ⚠ THIS CORRECTS A RECORDED CLAIM. Round 2026-09-16k-reftype's record and commit message attribute this
// over-admission to its `refptee` arm ("`let y: dyn Tr = *x` behind a `&(dyn Tr)` is ADMITTED ... an
// over-admission"), and cite it as one of the three reasons that arm must not land. The parenthesised
// spelling is unreachable on base only because the PARSER refuses it; the DEFECT is reachable on base with
// the plain `&dyn Tr` spelling, as this program shows. It is INHERITED, not caused by any arm (rule 14).
//
// Carrier differs from unsized_local_binds_place_dropped_after_free (tier 2), whose local's type is an
// unsized TYPE PARAMETER `T: ?Sized`; here the annotation is a concrete trait object and no generic is
// involved, so the two are not known to share a decision site.
package unsized_dyn_local_binds_deref_admits;
trait Tr { fn m(self: &Self) -> i64; }
struct S { v: i64 }
impl Tr for S { fn m(self: &Self) -> i64 { return self.v; } }
fn take(x: &dyn Tr) -> i64 {
    let y: dyn Tr = *x;
    return y.m();
}
fn main() -> i32 {
    let s: S = S { v: 1i64 };
    return (take(&s) - 1i64) as i32;
}
// rustc 1.98.1 --edition 2024: REFUSED, error[E0277]. Twin src/compiler/probes/2026-09-16l-refptee/rust/r13_illegal_unsized_behind_dynptee.rs
// (same defect one layer deeper, `&&dyn Tr`); the plain-`&dyn Tr` twin is rust/r15_illegal_unsized_plain_dynref.rs.
